winex11: tell the window manager when a dialog is modal.

Wine never sets _NET_WM_STATE_MODAL. The atom is not in the atom table at all,
and net_wm_state_atoms[] carries only six states. WM_TRANSIENT_FOR alone does
not oblige a window manager to keep a window above its parent -- MODAL is the
hint that does -- so a modal dialog can be stacked *below* the very window it
has disabled. The user then cannot find the dialog, the owner refuses input,
and the application appears to have frozen.

Observed with Affinity's Export dialog on Plasma 6: correct WM_TRANSIENT_FOR,
no MODAL, and the dialog sitting behind its owner.

Win32 modality needs no new flag to detect: a modal dialog is one whose owner
has been disabled for its duration, which is exactly what DialogBox and
IFileDialog::Show do.

Two things are needed beyond adding the atom:

  - the state must be recomputed when the OWNER's WS_DISABLED changes. It
    depends on the owner, not on the window itself, so nothing else triggers it:
    the dialog's state is otherwise computed once at creation, before the owner
    is disabled, and never corrected.

  - the enum in x11drv.h must stay in the same order as the name table in
    x11drv_main.c. X11DRV_Atoms[] is indexed by that enum, so inserting MODAL in
    a different position in each makes every atom from there on resolve to the
    wrong name -- setting MODAL then emits _NET_WM_STATE_SKIP_PAGER, which is
    both wrong and silent.

A disabled owner is a necessary condition for modality but nowhere near a
sufficient one, and taking it as sufficient costs the user the keyboard.

Every floating palette an application owns is owned by a disabled window for
as long as any dialog is up, so every one of them was advertised as modal.
Window.ShowDialog makes it worse by disabling all of the thread's top-level
windows rather than just the owner, and patch 0021 produces the same shape with
no Win32 dialog in it at all: the XDG portal chooser is the portal's own
window, and all Wine does is disable the owner for the duration.

A window manager that honours the hint then sends the owner's activation to a
palette. KWin does. Measured with a 90-line test program and no Affinity
involved, driving a main window, an owned WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW palette and a
dialog that comes and goes:

  before  dialog destroyed -> active = PALETTE, and it stays there after the
          owner is re-enabled and the state clears; the main window never gets
          the focus back
  after   dialog destroyed -> active = MAIN

The application is left running with the focus on a palette that does not
handle the keyboard, so typing and every shortcut do nothing until the user
alt-tabs away and back. Intermittent enough to look like individual shortcuts
failing one at a time.

So require two more things of a candidate:

  - it is not a tool window. A floating palette is one and a dialog is not --
    the same distinction this driver already draws for SC_MOVE. This is what
    rules out the palettes in the portal shape, where there is no dialog to
    prefer over them.

  - it is the owner's GW_ENABLEDPOPUP, the window Windows itself activates when
    a disabled owner is clicked. That is the modal dialog by definition, and it
    guarantees at most one window per owner claims the state.

GW_ENABLEDPOPUP is a property of the whole set of windows one owner owns, so
enabling or disabling any one of them can move it; a window whose WS_DISABLED
changes now refreshes its siblings as well as the windows it owns.

Verified across the three shapes an application can produce, counting samples
in which each window was advertised modal:

                     palette (wrong)   dialog (right)
  ShowDialog    old     153 / 506           91
                new       0 / 605          104
  DialogBox     old     161 / 564           96
                new       0 / 607          104
  portal 0021   old     183 / 614            -
                new       0 / 577            -

diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/window.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/window.c
index d105040..f1ed029 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/window.c
@@ -83,6 +83,7 @@ static const unsigned int net_wm_state_atoms[NB_NET_WM_STATES] =
     XATOM__NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE,
     XATOM__NET_WM_STATE_MAXIMIZED_VERT,
     XATOM__NET_WM_STATE_SKIP_PAGER,
+    XATOM__NET_WM_STATE_MODAL,
     XATOM__NET_WM_STATE_SKIP_TASKBAR
 };
 
@@ -1477,6 +1478,7 @@ static void update_net_wm_states( struct x11drv_win_data *data )
 {
     static const UINT fullscreen_mask = (1 << NET_WM_STATE_MAXIMIZED) | (1 << NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN);
     UINT style, ex_style, new_state = 0;
+    HWND owner;
 
     if (data->embedded) return;
     if (data->whole_window == root_window)
@@ -1500,6 +1502,38 @@ static void update_net_wm_states( struct x11drv_win_data *data )
     ex_style = NtUserGetWindowLongW( data->hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE );
     if (ex_style & WS_EX_TOPMOST)
         new_state |= (1 << NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE);
+
+    /* A modal dialog must stay above the window it blocks. WM_TRANSIENT_FOR
+     * alone does not oblige the window manager to do that -- _NET_WM_STATE_MODAL
+     * is the hint that does, and Wine has never set it. Without it a modal dialog
+     * can be stacked below its own owner, where the user cannot find it while the
+     * owner refuses input, so the application appears to have frozen.
+     *
+     * Win32 modality is not directly observable: there is no "is modal" flag to
+     * consult. A disabled owner is a necessary condition, but on its own it is
+     * far too weak -- it is equally true of every floating palette the
+     * application owns for as long as any dialog is up, and Window.ShowDialog
+     * disables every top-level window in the thread, not just the owner. Marking
+     * a palette modal is not cosmetic: the window manager sends the owner's
+     * activation to it instead, and since a palette is not where the application
+     * handles the keyboard, the application stops answering the keyboard until
+     * something re-activates it by hand.
+     *
+     * So require two more things of a candidate:
+     *
+     *  - it is not a tool window. A floating palette is one and a dialog is not;
+     *    this is the same distinction winex11 already draws in SC_MOVE handling.
+     *    It is what rules out the palettes when the owner is disabled and there
+     *    is no Win32 dialog at all, which is exactly the shape the XDG portal
+     *    file chooser produces.
+     *  - it is the owner's GW_ENABLEDPOPUP, the window Windows itself activates
+     *    when a disabled owner is clicked. That is the modal dialog by
+     *    definition, and it guarantees at most one window per owner claims the
+     *    state. */
+    if ((owner = NtUserGetWindowRelative( data->hwnd, GW_OWNER )) &&
+        !NtUserIsWindowEnabled( owner ) && !(ex_style & WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW) &&
+        NtUserGetWindowRelative( owner, GW_ENABLEDPOPUP ) == data->hwnd)
+        new_state |= (1 << NET_WM_STATE_MODAL);
     if (!data->add_taskbar)
     {
         if (data->skip_taskbar || (ex_style & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE)
@@ -2593,11 +2627,38 @@ void X11DRV_SetWindowText( HWND hwnd, LPCWSTR text )
  *
  * Update the X state of a window to reflect a style change
  */
+/* _NET_WM_STATE_MODAL on an owned window depends on its OWNER being disabled,
+ * not on anything about the window itself. So when a window's WS_DISABLED
+ * changes, the windows it owns have to be recomputed: their own styles have not
+ * changed, and nothing else will do it. Without this the state is computed once
+ * when the dialog is created -- before the owner is disabled -- and never
+ * corrected, so a modal dialog is never advertised as modal. */
+static void update_owned_windows_net_wm_states( HWND owner )
+{
+    HWND *list;
+    UINT i;
+
+    /* build_hwnd_list, as has_owned_popups uses: walking GW_CHILD from the
+     * desktop does not enumerate top-level windows. */
+    if (!(list = build_hwnd_list())) return;
+
+    for (i = 0; list[i] != HWND_BOTTOM; i++)
+    {
+        struct x11drv_win_data *data;
+
+        if (NtUserGetWindowRelative( list[i], GW_OWNER ) != owner) continue;
+        if (!(data = get_win_data( list[i] ))) continue;
+        if (data->whole_window) update_net_wm_states( data );
+        release_win_data( data );
+    }
+    free( list );
+}
+
 void X11DRV_SetWindowStyle( HWND hwnd, INT offset, STYLESTRUCT *style )
 {
     struct x11drv_win_data *data;
     DWORD changed = style->styleNew ^ style->styleOld;
-    BOOL flush = FALSE;
+    BOOL flush = FALSE, disabled_changed = FALSE;
 
     if (hwnd == NtUserGetDesktopWindow()) return;
     if (!(data = get_win_data( hwnd ))) return;
@@ -2607,6 +2668,7 @@ void X11DRV_SetWindowStyle( HWND hwnd, INT offset, STYLESTRUCT *style )
     {
         set_wm_hints( data );
         flush = TRUE;
+        disabled_changed = TRUE;
     }
 
     if (offset == GWL_EXSTYLE)
@@ -2624,6 +2686,20 @@ void X11DRV_SetWindowStyle( HWND hwnd, INT offset, STYLESTRUCT *style )
     if (flush) XFlush( data->display );
 done:
     release_win_data( data );
+
+    /* after release_win_data: these take the lock for each window they touch */
+    if (disabled_changed)
+    {
+        HWND owner;
+
+        update_owned_windows_net_wm_states( hwnd );
+        /* MODAL now depends on GW_ENABLEDPOPUP, which is a property of the whole
+         * set of windows one owner owns: enabling or disabling any one of them
+         * can move it to a different window. So refresh this window's siblings
+         * too, not only the windows it owns itself. */
+        if ((owner = NtUserGetWindowRelative( hwnd, GW_OWNER )))
+            update_owned_windows_net_wm_states( owner );
+    }
 }
 
 
